AC and SC problems — distinguish the joints (@shoulder-ac-sc: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @shoulder, #source @med-source-trauma, #source @med-source-arthritis, #medicine @shoulder-fractures, #orthopedics @shoulder-fractures, #shoulder @shoulder-fractures) { r_019WC3RKV49JT71GHN92X7S7D8
**Read:** [Shoulder fractures and dislocations — AAOS](../../../sources/Shoulder-trauma-AAOS.lingua#r_3PWVRKWPX21FH3M69BKW675ZHM) for traumatic relationships and [Arthritis of the shoulder — AAOS](../../../sources/Shoulder-arthritis-AAOS.lingua#r_7B3XNZ2YPTDVPMN3M6DH7R8S2M) for the distinction between AC and glenohumeral arthritis.

The acromioclavicular joint links the lateral clavicle and acromion. A 'separated shoulder' commonly concerns injury around this relationship and its supporting ligaments. It is different from dislocation of the humeral head from the glenoid.

The sternoclavicular joint links the medial clavicle with the sternum. Its location near important thoracic structures makes significant traumatic displacement a different clinical question from an isolated lateral shoulder complaint. Acute deformity or chest, breathing, or circulation symptoms after injury needs urgent assessment.

Arthritic change, ligament injury, and other processes can affect these joints. Pain reported near a joint is useful information but cannot by itself prove that joint is the source.

**Try:** locate AC and SC on a skeleton, draw the clavicle between them, and explain the supporting roles of the acromioclavicular, coracoclavicular, and costoclavicular ligaments. Translate a report that says 'AC' without silently converting it into a glenohumeral diagnosis.

